  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Friday)\nIssued at 213 PM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nCurrent Water Vapor and upper air models show southwest flow over\nthe forecast area, as upper low digs into the Rockies. 18Z surface\nanalysis depicts a low over southeastern SD, with frontal boundary\nstretching into central Nebraska. Winds are generally light out of\nthe north, with temperatures in the 60s to upper 70s. \n\nOut ahead of the upper low, a disturbance will lift up into the\nregion this evening. The line of showers/storms is currently in\ncentral Wyoming moving northeast, expected to enter the forecast\narea in a few hours. Generally light precipitation with the\nevening precipitation, but accumulations nearing a tenth of an\ninch may be possible in heavier precip cores. Precipitation may\nlinger into the overnight hours, and some showery development is\npossible again during the day Saturday. There's a low-end chance\nfor a severe storm Saturday afternoon, given some CAPE values \n500-1000 j/Kb, however weak effective shear (20-30kts). \n\nAttention then turns to the main event, as the upper low moves\nover Wyoming later Saturday into Sunday. Widespread precipitation\nis expected to move into the region over this timeframe. Heaviest\namounts are expected Sunday morning and afternoon and will taper\noff from west to east Sunday night into Monday. LREF probabilities\nfor 24 QPF 0.5\" or more has a bullseye of 70-90% over the Black\nHills into southwestern SD. Probabilities for 1\" or more QPF range\nfrom 30-60% in the same area. Expect cooler temperatures as well\nas the low moves into the plains, with high temperatures Sunday\nonly reaching around 60 over the plains, and the low to mid 50s\nover the Black Hills. \n\nBy the latter half of monday, the pattern changes again as upper \nridging builds over the plains, bringing a gradual warming trend \nMonday through much of next week. Temperatures climb back to at or\nabove normal by Tuesday and Wednesday.",
